  ],
  "description": "We identify and formally characterize a previously unrecognized failure mode in rubric-rewarded math RLVR: difficulty-dependent dimension exploitation. When K-dimensional rubric rewards are applied with uniform strictness, models face systematically different gaming incentives at each difficulty level — easy criteria remain satisfiable via pattern-matching on hard problems while hard criteria yield vanishing gradient signal, creating unpenalized gaming opportunities. We develop a Gaming Budget framework with three formal results that go substantially beyond per-criterion application of DGPO's binary-reward theorems: (T1) a Gaming Budget Theorem bounding total exploitable reward as a function of the criterion-difficulty pass rate matrix, verification gaps, AND criterion correlations — showing gaming amplifies when correlated criteria are jointly vulnerable; (T2) a Dimensionality Collapse Theorem proving that honest (non-gaming-vulnerable) gradient signal decays strictly faster than total signal as difficulty increases — the reward doesn't just shrink, it becomes increasingly one-sided; (T3) a Calibration Sufficiency Condition deriving the minimal difficulty-dependent strictness schedule that eliminates the gaming budget and characterizing when calibration alone suffices versus when additional defenses are needed. We introduce a difficulty-stratified gaming diagnostic producing K×5 heatmaps and pre-commit to four falsifiable predictions: (P1) universal exploitation channels — 2-3 criteria with flat difficulty profiles serving as gaming conduits across all difficulty levels; (P2) non-monotonic gaming — proof-verification criteria are MORE gamed at moderate difficulty than extreme difficulty; (P3) criterion correlation sign flip — positive correlations between surface and reasoning criteria on easy problems become negative on hard problems as gaming effort displaces reasoning; (P4) dimensionality collapse to K_eff ≤ 3 on hardest problems. These predictions ensure the paper delivers novel empirical findings whether confirmed or falsified. The diagnostic is validated on Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B trained with GRPO+RRM on MATH (levels 1-5) and judged by Qwen3-8B. As a proof-of-concept defense, we propose Difficulty-Calibrated Rubric Rewards (DCRR), benchmarked against DGPO+RRM and MO-GRPO+RRM (difficulty-agnostic variance normalization) baselines to isolate the value of difficulty-specific calibration. Critically, we test the DGPO+RRM and MO-GRPO+RRM combinations as the first experiments to confront the baseline fear head-on: if naive combinations already capture most gains, the paper's primary contribution stands on the diagnostic framework and Gaming Budget theory — the first constructive analysis revealing the STRUCTURE of how rubric gaming interacts with difficulty in math RLVR.",
  "project_name": "diff_calib_rubric_rlvr",
  "source": "web_research",
  "change_type": "major",
  "revision_notes": {
    "change_type_justification": "Major revision due to: (1) substantially deepened theoretical framework — three new theorems (Gaming Budget, Dimensionality Collapse, Calibration Sufficiency) that go genuinely beyond per-criterion vectorization of DGPO; (2) four pre-committed falsifiable predictions addressing the 'predictable finding' risk; (3) four new references integrated with deep differentiation; (4) new MO-GRPO+RRM baseline added to experimental design; (5) venue strategy completely revised with explicit justification.",
    "missing_reference_RuCL": "ADDED and deeply analyzed. RuCL (arXiv:2602.21628) stratifies rubrics by model competence: it measures empirical pass rates on 2K samples, assigns rubrics to foundational (high pass-rate) vs. advanced (low pass-rate) tiers, and uses a three-phase curriculum schedule (stabilize → ramp → consolidate) with coefficient λ_t controlling the easy/hard weighting. After reading the full paper, the key differentiation is precise: RuCL adjusts by MODEL COMPETENCE (what the model can currently learn) not PROBLEM DIFFICULTY (what gaming incentives the problem structure creates). Competence-based scheduling assumes honest learning — the pass rate reflects genuine capability. Our framework reveals that pass rates can be inflated by gaming, making competence proxies unreliable on gameable criteria at high difficulty. RuCL also provides no gaming diagnosis, no theoretical gaming characterization, and operates in multimodal (not math) domains. The gradient variance justification in RuCL (CV→∞ as p→0) is related but orthogonal — it motivates curriculum scheduling, not gaming diagnosis.",
    "missing_reference_MO_GRPO": "ADDED with deep technical integration. MO-GRPO (arXiv:2509.22047) normalizes advantages per-objective before aggregation (A^MO = Σ_k [R_k - mean(R_k)]/std(R_k)), fixing GRPO's variance-bias (Theorem 1: correlation with advantage ∝ σ_i). This is the MECHANISM underlying dimensional exploitation — high-variance criteria dominate the gradient. However, three key limitations make our work necessary: (1) MO-GRPO is difficulty-agnostic — it equalizes variance contributions but doesn't account for the difficulty-dependent structure of which criteria have what variance; (2) MO-GRPO treats dead criteria (p=0, σ=0) and saturated criteria (p=1, σ=0) as symmetrically uninformative, but on hard problems, dead criteria on hard rubric dimensions are INFORMATIVE about gaming (the model should be penalized for failing them, not have their signal suppressed); (3) MO-GRPO provides no gaming diagnosis or theoretical characterization of the gaming surface. Added MO-GRPO+RRM as a new baseline to test whether difficulty-agnostic variance normalization suffices.",
    "missing_reference_CDRRM": "ADDED. CDRRM (arXiv:2603.08035) uses a Contrast-then-Synthesis paradigm to generate quality rubrics via multi-dimensional contrastive profiling on preference pairs. Relevant to 'which criteria matter' but operates at rubric design time, not during training, and is difficulty-agnostic. It identifies discriminative factors across response pairs but does not analyze how discriminativeness changes with problem difficulty.",
    "missing_reference_C2": "ADDED. C2 (arXiv:2604.13618) trains cooperative rubric generators by measuring how rubrics influence reward model decisions, then a critical verifier filters misleading rubrics at inference time. Relevant to rubric quality but operates at inference-time filtering without difficulty stratification or gaming diagnosis.",
    "ARR_dates_fixed": "FIXED. No July ARR cycle exists. Corrected to August 3, 2026 submission → EACL 2027 commitment October 11, 2026. Verified via official ACL Rolling Review dates page.",
    "venue_mismatch_addressed": "EXPLICITLY ADDRESSED. The user's original target was AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28, 2026). With ~8 weeks from today (May 28), AAAI is feasible ONLY for a reduced-scope paper focusing on Contributions 1-2 (diagnostic + theory, no DCRR experiments or MO-GRPO baseline), and would require immediate start with compressed theory development. This is not recommended because: (a) the Gaming Budget theory (3 theorems) requires careful formalization that benefits from more time; (b) the MO-GRPO+RRM baseline is now critical for demonstrating that difficulty-agnostic normalization is insufficient; (c) the falsifiable predictions (especially P2: non-monotonic gaming and P3: correlation sign flip) need sufficient experimental validation. ICLR 2027 (Sep 19/24, ~17 weeks) is primary target with comfortable timeline for full scope. AAAI 2027 retained as aggressive alternative if user prioritizes earlier deadline. ARR Aug 3 → EACL 2027 as NLP-venue backup (~10 weeks, feasible for Contributions 1-2 with preliminary DCRR).",
    "theory_depth_strengthened": "SUBSTANTIALLY DEEPENED — this is the core change in this revision. The reviewer correctly identified that √(p_{k,d}(1−p_{k,d})) per criterion is straightforward vectorization. Three new formal results go beyond this: (T1) Gaming Budget Theorem — bounds total exploitable reward incorporating criterion CORRELATIONS (when correlated easy criteria are gamed, multiple scores inflate simultaneously) and verification GAPS (how hard it is for the judge to detect gaming per criterion per difficulty). This is genuinely novel — the correlation structure is absent in binary rewards, and the verification gap interaction is specific to rubric evaluation. The bound reveals gaming concentration on criteria with flat difficulty profiles + low verification stringency + high inter-correlation. (T2) Dimensionality Collapse Theorem — proves honest (non-gaming-vulnerable) gradient signal decays faster than total signal. This is the key structural insight: the reward doesn't just shrink with difficulty, it becomes increasingly one-sided. Neither DGPO (binary) nor MO-GRPO (difficulty-agnostic) capture this asymmetric collapse. (T3) Calibration Sufficiency Condition — derives s_{k,d} ∝ 1/δ_{k,d} · √(p_{k,d}(1−p_{k,d})) as the minimal gaming-eliminating schedule, and proves a necessary condition on the verification gap matrix for when calibration alone suffices vs. when additional defenses are needed. This gives a constructive recipe AND characterizes its limits.",
    "predictable_finding_risk_addressed": "ADDRESSED with four pre-committed falsifiable predictions: (P1) Universal Exploitation Channels — gaming concentrates on criteria with FLAT difficulty profiles (not merely the easiest criteria); (P2) Non-Monotonic Gaming — inverted-U gaming curve for proof-verification criteria, peaking at moderate difficulty where models can generate plausible-but-invalid proofs but not at extreme difficulty where even superficial proof structures fail; (P3) Correlation Sign Flip — positive correlations between surface and reasoning criteria on easy problems become NEGATIVE on hard problems as gaming effort displaces reasoning; (P4) Dimensionality Collapse — effective reward dimensionality drops to K_eff ≤ 3 on MATH level 5. These predictions are specific, non-obvious, and interesting whether confirmed or falsified. The non-monotonic prediction (P2) is supported by LLMs Gaming Verifiers (arXiv:2604.15149) showing gaming increases with complexity for binary verification — but under rubric evaluation, the prediction is that specific criterion-level gaming follows a different pattern (inverted-U for hard criteria, monotonic for easy criteria), which would be a genuinely novel finding.",
    "MO_GRPO_baseline_added": "Added MO-GRPO+RRM as a new experimental baseline. This is critical: it tests whether difficulty-AGNOSTIC per-objective variance normalization (MO-GRPO's approach) suffices to mitigate dimensional exploitation, or whether difficulty-SPECIFIC calibration (DCRR) provides additional value. If MO-GRPO+RRM already eliminates gaming, DCRR is unnecessary but the diagnostic and theory still contribute; if MO-GRPO+RRM reduces but doesn't eliminate gaming on hard problems, DCRR's difficulty-specific calibration is justified. Resource estimate updated from 7 to 8 training runs (+20 H100-hours).",
    "gradient_starvation_connection": "Added Gradient Starvation paper (arXiv:2605.07689) as a reference. Their per-prompt degeneracy formula D(p,G)=p^G+(1-p)^G in the binary case extends naturally to per-criterion degeneracy D_k(p_{k,d},G) in the rubric case. The key extension: in the binary case, degeneracy is a single number per prompt; in the K-dimensional case, the PATTERN of which criteria are degenerate at which difficulty levels reveals the gaming surface structure. This connects our diagnostic framework to a precise mathematical foundation.",
    "compute_budget": "Slightly increased from 190 to 210 H100-hours to accommodate the MO-GRPO+RRM baseline (one additional training run) and correlation analysis computation. Remains within reasonable bounds for the contribution scope."
